================================
  ARCHITECTURE AUDIT REPORT
  ================================
  Project: code-smells-project
  Stack:   Python 3 + Flask 3.1.1
  Files:   4 analyzed | ~784 lines of code

  ## Summary
  CRITICAL: 9 | HIGH: 7 | MEDIUM: 6 | LOW: 4
  Total findings: 26

  ## Findings
  (ordered CRITICAL → HIGH → MEDIUM → LOW; within same severity, ordered by file)

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] Hardcoded SECRET_KEY in Source
  File: app.py:7
  Description: `app.config["SECRET_KEY"] = "minha-chave-super-secreta-123"` is written directly in source and committed
  to VCS.
  Impact: Anyone with repo access can forge sessions/tokens; secret rotation requires a redeploy and code change.
  Recommendation: Move to `config/settings.py` and load via `os.environ.get("SECRET_KEY")`; document in `.env.example`.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] Hardcoded DEBUG=True in Production Boot
  File: app.py:8, app.py:88
  Description: `app.config["DEBUG"] = True` and `app.run(..., debug=True)` are hardcoded, enabling the Werkzeug
  interactive debugger.
  Impact: In production this exposes a remote code-execution console via the debugger PIN-guess; even with the PIN, full
   stack traces are leaked to clients.
  Recommendation: Drive from `Config.DEBUG = os.environ.get("DEBUG", "false").lower() == "true"`.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] Arbitrary SQL Execution Endpoint (Auth-less)
  File: app.py:59-78
  Description: `POST /admin/query` reads `dados.get("sql", "")` from the request body and runs it verbatim via
  `cursor.execute(query)`, returning all rows. No authentication, no allowlist, no rate-limit.
  Impact: Any unauthenticated client can read, modify, or drop every table — full database compromise.
  Recommendation: Remove this endpoint entirely. If a maintenance tool is needed, gate it behind an authenticated admin
  role with a parameterized, allowlisted command set.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] Destructive Admin Endpoint Without Auth
  File: app.py:47-57
  Description: `POST /admin/reset-db` deletes every row in `itens_pedido`, `pedidos`, `produtos`, and `usuarios` with no
   authentication, CSRF protection, or confirmation.
  Impact: Trivial denial-of-service / total data loss by any anonymous caller.
  Recommendation: Either delete this endpoint or wrap it in an authenticated middleware that requires an admin role;
  expose only in a dev-only blueprint registered when `DEBUG=true`.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] Hardcoded SECRET_KEY Leaked Through Health Endpoint
  File: controllers.py:289
  Description: `health_check()` returns `"secret_key": "minha-chave-super-secreta-123"` in the JSON response body. The
  secret is duplicated in source and broadcast on every health probe.
  Impact: Even if the secret were rotated, the endpoint would still publish it; any external monitor / proxy / log
  aggregator captures it.
  Recommendation: Remove `secret_key`, `db_path`, and `debug` keys from the health response; never echo configuration
  values.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] Plain-Text Passwords in Storage and Comparison
  File: database.py:75-83, models.py:105-120, models.py:122-131
  Description: Seed users insert `"admin123"`, `"123456"`, `"senha123"` directly into `usuarios.senha`. `login_usuario`
  compares the raw input against the stored value (`WHERE email = '...' AND senha = '...'`). No hashing of any kind.
  Impact: A single SELECT (or the SQL-injection vector below) exposes every credential in clear text.
  Recommendation: Hash with `bcrypt` on create (`bcrypt.hashpw`) and verify with `bcrypt.checkpw`. Add `bcrypt>=4.0` to
  `requirements.txt`. Re-seed dev fixtures with hashed values.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] SQL Injection via String Concatenation Throughout Model Layer
  File: models.py:28, 47-50, 57-60, 68, 92, 109-110, 126-129, 140, 148-150, 155, 157-160, 163-166, 174, 188, 192, 220,
  224, 280-281, 289-297
  Description: Every query is built with string concatenation of user-controlled values. Examples: `"SELECT * FROM
  produtos WHERE id = " + str(id)` (line 28), `"SELECT * FROM usuarios WHERE email = '" + email + "' AND senha = '" +
  senha + "'"` (lines 109-110), and a search builder that concatenates `termo`, `categoria`, `preco_min`, `preco_max`
  (lines 289-297).
  Impact: Authentication bypass on `/login` (e.g. `' OR '1'='1`), data exfiltration, and arbitrary mutation are trivial.
  Recommendation: Replace every interpolation with placeholders: `cursor.execute("SELECT ... WHERE id = ?", (id,))`.
  Centralize per-entity in new model classes.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] God File — models.py Mixes Four Domains and Business Logic
  File: models.py:1-315
  Description: A single 315-line module contains DB access for `produtos`, `usuarios`, `pedidos`, `itens_pedido`, plus
  business rules (discount tiers in `relatorio_vendas`, stock validation and total computation in `criar_pedido`). No
  separation of entities.
  Impact: Any change risks breaking unrelated domains; impossible to unit-test one entity in isolation.
  Recommendation: Split into `models/produto_model.py`, `models/usuario_model.py`, `models/pedido_model.py`,
  `models/item_pedido_model.py`. Move discount and checkout business rules to controllers.

  ---

  ### [CRITICAL] God File — controllers.py Mixes Five Domains
  File: controllers.py:1-293
  Description: Single 293-line file holds handlers for produtos, usuários, login, pedidos, relatórios, and health.
  Validation, business orchestration, and side-effects (print "ENVIANDO EMAIL/SMS/PUSH") are all interleaved.
  Impact: High coupling, repeated try/except scaffolding, and any new domain forces edits in the same file — merge
  conflicts and accidental cross-domain regressions.
  Recommendation: Split into `controllers/produto_controller.py`, `controllers/usuario_controller.py`,
  `controllers/pedido_controller.py`, etc., and pair each with a `views/<entity>_routes.py` Blueprint.

  ---

  ### [HIGH] Global Mutable DB Connection (Singleton)
  File: database.py:4-11
  Description: `db_connection = None` is a module-level global; `get_db()` lazily initializes it with
  `check_same_thread=False` and stores it on the global. All callers share one connection.
  Impact: No dependency injection — every model/controller is hard-bound to this singleton, making unit tests impossible
   without monkey-patching. `check_same_thread=False` plus a single shared connection causes race conditions under
  concurrent requests.
  Recommendation: Use Flask's `g` object with `teardown_appcontext`: one connection per request. Build a
  `models/database.py` factory and inject the connection via `get_db()` from within request context.

  ---

  ### [HIGH] Passwords Returned in Listing & Detail Endpoints
  File: controllers.py:128-134, controllers.py:136-144, models.py:72-87, models.py:89-103
  Description: `GET /usuarios` and `GET /usuarios/<id>` build dicts that include `"senha": row["senha"]` directly from
  the model. Any caller of the API can read every user's password.
  Impact: Complete credential disclosure even without SQL injection; trivially exploitable.
  Recommendation: Remove `senha` from the user serializer in the model. Define a `to_safe_dict` that exposes only `id`,
  `nome`, `email`, `tipo`, `criado_em`.

  ---

  ### [HIGH] Business Logic in Model Layer (relatorio_vendas)
  File: models.py:235-273
  Description: `relatorio_vendas()` runs five count/sum queries then applies tiered-discount business rules (`if
  faturamento > 10000: desconto = faturamento * 0.1` …) and computes `ticket_medio` — pure business logic mixed with
  data access.
  Impact: Discount rules cannot be unit-tested without hitting the database; rule changes require touching a SQL file.
  Recommendation: Keep aggregate queries in `PedidoModel.get_sales_aggregates()`; move discount and ticket-médio
  calculations to `PedidoController.build_sales_report()` with thresholds/rates read from `config`.

  ---

  ### [HIGH] Business Logic + Multi-Step Orchestration in Model (criar_pedido)
  File: models.py:133-169
  Description: Model method performs stock validation, total computation, three sequential mutations (insert pedido,
  insert itens, decrement estoque), and returns either a result or an `{"erro": ...}` envelope — business rules and
  persistence intermixed in a single function with no transaction guarantees.
  Impact: Failure mid-way leaves partial writes (no rollback); business rule "stock must cover quantity" is untestable
  without DB; error shape leaks into the controller.
  Recommendation: Move orchestration to `PedidoController.process_checkout()`; have the model expose narrow methods
  (`get_by_id`, `create_pedido`, `create_item`, `decrement_estoque`) and wrap the whole flow in a `with db: ...`
  transaction. Raise typed exceptions instead of returning error dicts.

  ---

  ### [HIGH] Validation Logic Embedded in Controller (criar_produto / atualizar_produto)
  File: controllers.py:24-62, controllers.py:64-96
  Description: Each handler contains ~30 lines of inline validation (presence, negativity, length range, hard-coded
  category allowlist) before calling the model. The exact same block is duplicated in `atualizar_produto`.
  Impact: Validation rules drift between create and update; cannot be tested separately from the HTTP layer.
  Recommendation: Extract to `ProdutoController.validate_payload(data)` returning `(cleaned, error)`. Allow controllers
  to focus on orchestration.

  ---

  ### [HIGH] Side-Effects (Notifications) Triggered Inside Controllers
  File: controllers.py:208-210, controllers.py:247-250
  Description: `criar_pedido` and `atualizar_status_pedido` emit notifications with `print("ENVIANDO EMAIL: …")` /
  `print("ENVIANDO SMS: …")` directly in the controller after persistence.
  Impact: Notifications cannot be disabled in tests, batched, or replaced with a real provider without editing the
  controller. Pure side-effect concerns leak into the HTTP layer.
  Recommendation: Extract to a `services/notification_service.py` (or controller helper `_notify_order_created`),
  invoked from the controller. Even keeping it as a print, isolating the call is enough to make it mockable.

  ---

  ### [HIGH] Admin Routes Defined Inline in app.py with Direct DB Access
  File: app.py:47-78
  Description: `/admin/reset-db` and `/admin/query` are declared via `@app.route` inside the composition root and call
  `cursor.execute()` directly — bypassing the model layer entirely.
  Impact: `app.py` accumulates business behaviour, violating its role as a thin entrypoint.
  Recommendation: Either remove (recommended) or relocate to a guarded `views/admin_routes.py` Blueprint and call new
  model methods.

