  ### [MEDIUM] N+1 Query in get_pedidos_usuario
  File: models.py:171-201
  Description: One SELECT fetches all orders for a user, then for each order another SELECT loads items, then for each
  item a third SELECT fetches the product name — three nested levels.
  Impact: A user with 20 orders averaging 5 items each costs 1 + 20 + 100 = 121 queries; latency grows linearly with
  order count.
  Recommendation: Replace with a single LEFT JOIN across `pedidos`, `itens_pedido`, `produtos`; group rows in Python by
  `pedido_id`.

  ---

  ### [MEDIUM] N+1 Query in get_todos_pedidos
  File: models.py:203-233
  Description: Same N+1 pattern as above, but across all orders system-wide.
  Impact: O(orders × items) queries per request — degrades the order-list endpoint as data grows.
  Recommendation: Same JOIN strategy; alternatively, batch-fetch items by `pedido_id IN (...)` then product names in a
  second IN-query.

  ---

  ### [MEDIUM] No Centralized Error Handler
  File: app.py (entire), controllers.py:5-292
  Description: There is no `@app.errorhandler` registration; every controller wraps its body in `try / except Exception
  as e: return jsonify({"erro": str(e)}), 500`. `str(e)` may leak internal details, and inconsistencies are inevitable.
  Impact: Stack traces / DB-driver error strings can reach clients; new handlers easily forget the boilerplate, breaking
   the contract.
  Recommendation: Add `middlewares/error_handler.py` registering 400/404/422/500/Exception handlers via
  `register_error_handlers(app)`. Remove per-handler `try/except Exception` and let exceptions propagate.

  ---

  ### [MEDIUM] Duplicated Validation Block (Produto Create vs Update)
  File: controllers.py:24-62, controllers.py:64-96
  Description: The presence checks, type/range checks, and JSON-parsing boilerplate are duplicated almost verbatim
  between `criar_produto` and `atualizar_produto`.
  Impact: Two places to change a rule; rules already differ slightly (create checks `len(nome) > 200`, update does not).
  Recommendation: Extract to `ProdutoController._validate(data, partial: bool)` returning a normalized payload or
  raising `ValueError`.

  ---

  ### [MEDIUM] Duplicated Row-to-Dict Mapping
  File: models.py:10-22, 30-40, 78-86, 95-102, 178-185, 211-217, 302-313
  Description: The same `{"id": row["id"], "nome": row["nome"], ...}` dict construction is repeated in seven places
  (produtos and pedidos serializers, with subtle variations).
  Impact: Schema additions/renames require updating multiple sites; bugs introduced when one site is missed.
  Recommendation: Add a single `to_dict()` static method per model class.

  ---

  ### [MEDIUM] Repeated try/except Boilerplate in Every Controller Handler
  File: controllers.py:5-292
  Description: 14 of 14 handlers wrap their body in `try / except Exception as e: return jsonify({"erro": str(e)}),
  500`. Once the centralized handler is added (above), every handler can drop the wrapper.
  Impact: Visual noise and a single class of error-handling that hides root causes.
  Recommendation: Delete the wrapper after registering global handlers; let exceptions bubble to the middleware.

  ---

  ### [LOW] Magic Numbers in Discount Tiers
  File: models.py:257-262
  Description: Tiers `> 10000`, `> 5000`, `> 1000` and rates `0.1`, `0.05`, `0.02` are inline literals with no
  explanation.
  Impact: Business rules are invisible to anyone scanning config; tuning requires a code change.
  Recommendation: Define `SALES_REPORT_TIERS` (list of tuples) in `config/settings.py`; iterate to pick the applicable
  tier.

  ---

  ### [LOW] Magic String Lists (Status / Categoria)
  File: controllers.py:52 (categorias_validas), controllers.py:242 (status list)
  Description: Allowed categories `["informatica", "moveis", "vestuario", "geral", "eletronicos", "livros"]` and order
  statuses `["pendente", "aprovado", "enviado", "entregue", "cancelado"]` are inline literals.
  Impact: Cannot change list without code edit; same list redeclared on each request.
  Recommendation: Move to `config/settings.py` (or an enum); reference everywhere via `Config.CATEGORIAS_VALIDAS`.

  ---

  ### [LOW] print() Used as Application Logging
  File: app.py:56, app.py:84-86, controllers.py:8, 11, 57, 61, 106, 161, 179, 182, 208-210, 219, 248, 250
  Description: Status messages and errors are written with `print(...)` to stdout instead of a structured logger.
  Impact: No log levels, no destination control, no correlation IDs; production observability is poor.
  Recommendation: Use `logging.getLogger(__name__)` per module; configure once in `app.py`.

  ---

  ### [LOW] No Input Validation on Login Payload Beyond Presence
  File: controllers.py:167-186
  Description: `login()` checks only that `email` and `senha` are non-empty, then concatenates them straight into a SQL
  string in the model. No length/format checks.
  Impact: Combined with the SQL injection above, missing validation widens the attack surface.
  Recommendation: After fixing SQL with placeholders, validate `email` against a simple regex and reject obviously
  malformed values with `400`.

